  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\" and \"A candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable; one that does not (a float, for instance) is passed over\". Reply A is the text: Here is my answer: ```json\\n{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":2}\\n```\\n{\"c\":3}. Reply B is the text: {\"c\":3}. For Reply A the rule requires the answer {\"b\":2}, because the fence holds two objects and the last one inside the fence is {\"b\":2}. For Reply B the rule requires {\"c\":3}, because there is no fence and the last top-level object is {\"c\":3}. The function, however, returns {\"c\":3} for both: in Reply A the fenced span fails to parse as one object, so the code falls through to the top-level pool and scores the last top-level object, which is {\"c\":3}. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if recover_json_object(Reply A) returned {\"b\":2} or any answer other than {\"c\":3}, or if recover_json_object(Reply B) returned anything other than {\"c\":3}, or if the rule is read so that a fence holding two objects does not count as \"any fence holds one\" and the fallback is correct."
